Build and run QEMU's venus device on a Darwin host, and let it present.

QEMU treats venus as part of its OpenGL story, and on macOS there is no
OpenGL story to be part of: libepoxy there exposes no EGL, so QEMU decides it
has no OpenGL, and every gate that says "virgl and opengl" closes. venus needs
none of it — it forwards Vulkan to a host driver and hands back buffers, and
never makes a GL context.

Building it:

- the virtio-gpu-gl device and its PCI variant are built when virglrenderer is
  present, without also demanding OpenGL, on Darwin;
- the renderer's EGL references — a display callback handed to virglrenderer
  when a GL display exists — compile out with CONFIG_OPENGL;
- realize no longer refuses a venus device for want of a GL-capable display,
  which would make it unusable on macOS and refuse a headless run everywhere;
- and a venus device without a GL display initializes the Vulkan renderer
  alone. On a host with OpenGL that is asked of `display_opengl`, which
  system/system.h declares. Asking virglrenderer for the GL renderer as well ends in an assertion
  the moment it calls back to create a context.

Showing what it renders:

- the device stops telling the console it needs a GL context. That flag is what
  the GL renderer needs, not what virgl means: a venus device on a host without
  OpenGL renders through Vulkan and presents a mappable blob, and a display
  backend shows that as an ordinary surface. Advertising the requirement
  refuses, before anything is drawn, a device that would have worked.
- a blob scanout no longer requires a dmabuf. A dmabuf is how a Linux host
  shares the rendered image with the display backend; a host that has none can
  still show it, because the resource is mappable and its pixels are in memory
  this process can address. That is the same path the 2D device already takes
  for a blob — reached here because the renderer's memory arrives as shared
  memory rather than as a descriptor, which is what a host whose Vulkan driver
  imports pointers rather than exporting them gives back. A blob is shown
  through its mapping whenever the console has no GL, since only a GL console
  imports a dmabuf; one the guest mapped is read through that mapping, and a
  scanout showing it is disabled before the mapping goes.
- 2D resources, which the renderer cannot make without its GL renderer, are
  made, filled and shown by the device as the 2D device does, so a guest's
  dumb buffers (the boot splash) reach the display. They are answered at
  once, fence and all: without its GL renderer virglrenderer signals no fence
  on context 0. A virgl SET_SCANOUT that turns a scanout off also forgets
  the surface a 2D resource was shown in, which the console has just freed.
  Otherwise the next 2D SET_SCANOUT reads freed memory and crashes the
  emulator, which the restart boot did when it killed the display server and
  started another.

Also needs --disable-pvg at configure time on macOS 27, where Apple's own
ParavirtualizedGraphics device does not compile: PGTask_t is obsoleted there.
That is unrelated to this stack and is a configure flag rather than a patch.

